1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,360
 In this video, we're going to talk about the design of forms for manufacturing FRP composites.

2
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:08,880
 While there are lots of different processes you can use to lay out fiber reinforced polymers,

3
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:14,400
 all of them rely on a form, a geometry, a rock upon which you build your FRP.

4
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:18,640
 We generally refer to positive or male forms as plugs or bucks,

5
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:22,480
 and we generally refer to female or negative forms as molds.

6
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:27,280
 But in either case, a well executed FRP layup is going to see a part made that replicates

7
00:00:27,280 --> 00:00:29,840
 the form geometry and surface finish perfectly.

8
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:32,640
 And so ensuring that the geometry and the surface finish of your form

9
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:37,200
 faithfully represent the geometry and the surface finish that you'd like to make is critically important.

10
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:41,200
 But on top of that, there are two key considerations you need to make in the design of a form.

11
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:45,440
 The first is ensuring that the form geometry or the part geometry that informs the form

12
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:50,000
 geometry doesn't have any features that would mean that the part is trapped or entombed

13
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,120
 within the form and can't be removed after it's cured.

14
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:55,600
 And the second is ensuring that the preparation of the form,

15
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:59,200
 both the surface finish and the application of any kind of release agent,

16
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:03,120
 is such that the part doesn't become stuck to the form and can't be removed.

17
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:06,800
 We'll begin by talking about geometry, because when you lay up an FRP,

18
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:12,240
 the ingredients that you put in are all kind of discontinuous and relatively soft and flexible

19
00:01:12,240 --> 00:01:16,080
 in terms of the fibers or illiquid in terms of the resin, it's actually very much possible

20
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:21,600
 to lay up a part inside of a mold or over a plug that once cured can't be removed.

21
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:25,120
 This happens if the form has what we call reentrant geometry where it kind of

22
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:26,960
 goes in on itself and back out again.

23
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:31,280
 Because generally speaking, there's no way to get the part up and over and outside of the form

24
00:01:31,280 --> 00:01:34,880
 after it's cured without damage to either the form or the part.

25
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:38,240
 Now, this isn't always the case. Sometimes the part or the form are flexible enough that

26
00:01:38,240 --> 00:01:42,880
 you can just squeeze them by, but generally speaking, we try to avoid re-entrant

27
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:46,080
 geometries in the design of parts that are going to be made this way.

28
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:49,040
 In fact, we normally go the other way, ensuring there's a little bit of what we call

29
00:01:49,040 --> 00:01:53,760
 draft on our parts, a taper towards the outside of the mold or the outside of the form,

30
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:57,440
 where a re-entrant geometry can't be avoided, so you've got some shape that you need to make

31
00:01:57,440 --> 00:02:01,280
 and you can't compromise on that shape for the sake of manufacturing convenience.

32
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:04,080
 Then you might elect to use something like a two-part mold.

33
00:02:04,080 --> 00:02:07,680
 Here, two independent halves come together to make up the full geometry in their

34
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:10,640
 clamped or glued or bolted together for the sake of layup.

35
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:14,000
 After the part is cured inside of the mold, for example, it can be unbolted and pulled

36
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:15,600
 apart and the part can be removed.

37
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:18,960
 This can be quite convenient, but obviously comes with the initial complexity and design

38
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:23,840
 and manufacture initially to make a two-part form. If you're not careful, you can also get

39
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:27,120
 a bit of a witness line where the two molds came together.

40
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:30,880
 The second key consideration for your forms is ensuring that they're adequately prepared

41
00:02:30,880 --> 00:02:34,880
 to make sure that the part doesn't stick to the form and can't be removed.

42
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:38,400
 To be able to actually remove a part from a form, you need to kind of break the seal

43
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:42,800
 between the part and the form, between the resin that's used in the part and the form.

44
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:47,440
 And all thermosany resins by their very nature provide some level of adhesion.

45
00:02:47,440 --> 00:02:50,800
 In fact, this adhesion is what allows our parts to take the shape of the form.

46
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:53,200
 To make sure you get clean separation between the part and the form,

47
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:54,560
 there are a couple of things you need to do.

48
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:58,720
 The first is ensure that the form has as good a surface finish as you can afford before you

49
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:03,040
 lay up your parts. In doing so, you knock down all of the surface roughness that provides

50
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:06,880
 a nice place for all of that resin to bite and you make releasing the part a bit easier,

51
00:03:06,880 --> 00:03:09,200
 as well as improving the surface finish of that part.

52
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:13,120
 The second is carefully preparing the form with some kind of release agent before lay up.

53
00:03:13,120 --> 00:03:16,880
 Wax that's kind of polished on and PVA that's slightly brushed or sprayed on a common

54
00:03:16,880 --> 00:03:21,920
 example is in both of these provide some level of separation between the resin and the form

55
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:24,320
 such that it's easy to remove one from the other.

56
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:28,080
 You can make forms for FRP lay up out of a variety of different materials.

57
00:03:28,080 --> 00:03:31,920
 At the hobbyist or single-use level, it's common to see forms made from timber and

58
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:36,480
 polystyrene foam and bits of sheet aluminium. And even at this production scale without the

59
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:41,520
 help of CNC machining tools, you can make some pretty organic, complicated 3D geometries with

60
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:46,000
 a reasonable level of accuracy. You can do this for example by building up skeleton templates

61
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,720
 out of timber and filling them with polystyrene foam. Then cutting this foam with a hot wire cutter

62
00:03:50,720 --> 00:03:54,560
 and bogging and sanding and polishing until you get that mirror finish.

63
00:03:54,560 --> 00:03:58,560
 Of course, in this approach, the dimensional accuracy of the form and the quality of the finish

64
00:03:58,560 --> 00:04:02,640
 you get is largely a function of the skill and the patience of the operator.

65
00:04:02,640 --> 00:04:06,160
 And to be honest, production of quality forms this way can be a little bit of an art.

66
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:10,640
 People who are really good at it, for example, have generally practiced for years and years.

67
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:13,680
 At the other end of the spectrum, for production scale parts or even for

68
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:17,120
 one-off parts that need to be a little bit more accurate or one-off parts that you don't want to

69
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:21,440
 invest all of the time and effort in building a form for. It's very common, for example, to use

70
00:04:21,440 --> 00:04:26,880
 a CNC machine to mill out some polystyrene or modeling foam to make up a mold or a plug.

71
00:04:26,880 --> 00:04:30,080
 These can be quite a bit more expensive, but the results kind of speak for themselves.

72
00:04:30,080 --> 00:04:34,160
 You can get results that are as accurate as any other machining process. And while it is a little

73
00:04:34,160 --> 00:04:38,480
 bit slow going, because you're cutting foam, you can run at pretty high feeds and speeds.

74
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:42,400
 Now, whether you aim to use a form that is a mold or a plug, it's largely a function of the

75
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:46,640
 geometry that you'd like to make and how important it is that the surface finish is good on

76
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:50,960
 particular faces on that geometry. Generally speaking, you'll get a better surface finish on the

77
00:04:50,960 --> 00:04:55,680
 side of your lab that's touching the form, whether that be a mold or a plug. In other words,

78
00:04:55,680 --> 00:05:00,160
 if you're using a mold, then when you pull your part out, the outside of that part is going to

79
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:04,000
 have a better surface finish. And if you're using a plug, when you pull the part off the plug,

80
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:08,800
 the inside of the part is going to have a better surface finish. In any case, the side of your lab

81
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:13,200
 that doesn't touch the form, the backside generally has a poorer surface finish. And balancing

82
00:05:13,200 --> 00:05:17,280
 this consideration of trying to maintain a good surface finish on the side of your part that you

83
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:22,400
 care about alongside the difficulty associated with manufacturer form is a complicated one.

84
00:05:22,400 --> 00:05:26,560
 By that, I mean that it's generally easier to sand and work and refine a positive geometry,

85
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:31,360
 a plug than it is to try and refine a negative geometry because sanding a concave shape is

86
00:05:31,360 --> 00:05:35,680
 generally harder than sanding a convex shape. But if you want the outside of your part to have a

87
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:40,800
 good surface finish, then you generally want a mold, a negative geometry over a positive one.

88
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:44,960
 In these circumstances, it's actually quite common to build a plug form, a positive geometry,

89
00:05:44,960 --> 00:05:50,480
 and lay up a mold over the top of that plug. In other words, make your mold out of an FRP.

90
00:05:50,480 --> 00:05:55,280
 Then once that mold's removed from the plug, it can be very, very lightly refined before finally

91
00:05:55,280 --> 00:06:00,000
 laying the part up inside the mold to get a good surface finish on the outside of the part.

92
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,400
 In some cases, if you end up with both a male plug and a female mold and the geometry permits,

93
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:08,880
 you can actually use both of these together to try and get a good surface finish on both sides

94
00:06:08,880 --> 00:06:12,800
 of your part. Here, you kind of lay up on the inside of the female mold and then bring the

95
00:06:12,800 --> 00:06:18,080
 male plug in behind to sand which you'll lay up between two sides of a nice surface.

96
00:06:18,080 --> 00:06:22,480
 Obviously, in these circumstances, to ensure that the parts actually fit up, you need to adjust

97
00:06:22,480 --> 00:06:27,760
 the sizes of your male and female molds and plugs respectively to account for the thickness of your

98
00:06:27,760 --> 00:06:32,160
 layout. I've left some really, really good videos that talk more about this on the textbook page

99
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:36,800
 for this one. Videos that talk about building molds and plugs out of 3D prints, as well as videos

100
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:40,960
 that talk about doing it kind of the old-fashioned way with skeleton structures. I hope you enjoy them.
